// Concurrent collection on the Windows thread provider (#4247, closing the
// gap #4060 left open: "Windows is compiled and linked ... but NOT RUN").
//
// A DELIBERATE SIBLING OF ../../gcthreadsdarwin AND ../../gcthreadslinux, NOT
// A THIRD TOP-LEVEL STRESS-CORPUS ENTRY. `_tests_/bit/stress/stress.bit`'s
// walker recognizes exactly two skip markers, `darwin-only` and `linux-only`
// (see its own header at the `target != targetArm64Macos` check) — there is
// no `windows-only` marker, and this harness never runs with a Windows
// build target in the first place, so a marker would never fire. Placing
// this program at the top level of `_tests_/stress/` with no way to skip it
// would make `test-stress-batch` try to natively compile
// `extern fn CreateThread`/`WaitOnAddress` on whichever host runs the gate
// (Darwin or x86_64-linux) and fail outright — the same E0078 the
// `darwin-only`/`linux-only` header already documents for the other two
// providers' externs.
//
// So this lives as a SUBDIRECTORY of ../.. (`_tests_/stress/thread/`), the
// same way ../props, ../exitprops and ../gcprops already do: a directory is
// only a Bit module when something imports it (`compiler/project.bit`'s own
// module contract), and nothing reachable from `../../thread.bit` or from
// any Darwin/Linux corpus program imports `./` here — so `test-stress-batch`
// never loads, never compiles, and never runs this file. It is a HAND-RUN
// fixture only, built and shipped with:
//
//   BIT_LIBBITRT=<archive> bit build _tests_/stress/thread/gcthreadswindows \
//     --target x86_64-windows -o <out>.exe
//
// and executed on real Windows hardware over SSH (`mustafa-desktop-win`, the
// only Windows box in this workspace — see the `desktop-win-and-wsl-are-
// one-machine` note: `mustafa-desktop-wsl` is the SAME PHYSICAL MACHINE and
// WSL interop can silently run a `.exe` through translation, so a gate must
// assert something that differs between the two, not just exit 0).
//
// THE PROPERTY UNDER TEST, and the argument for why it discriminates, lives
// in ../gcprops (`gcThreadsRun`/`gcThreadsTotal`) — identical logic to the
// Darwin and Linux twins: two raw OS threads (never through `spawn`, so
// neither is a scheduler task the way `runtime/sched`'s workers are, which
// already called the registration entry before #4060) run ordinary,
// non-`@nosplit`, ALLOCATING Bit code, each holding a live `[]i64(16)` across
// every collection `BIT_GC=stress` forces and re-verifying it element by
// element afterwards. `runtime/thread/windows/spawn.bit`'s
// `threadTrampoline` is the ONLY thing that puts either child thread's
// mutator slot in the registry — #4060 moved `bit_rt_gc_thread_enter()`
// there as the trampoline's first statement, off the old per-`gcAlloc` fast
// path. If that call is missing, `worldStoppedFor` never waits for the
// thread (an unclaimed slot reads as already-stopped) and
// `stwScanOwnStack` has no published stack range to scan for it — a
// collection that lands while a child is running sweeps its `keep` array out
// from under it, and G3 in `gcThreadsRun`'s bitmask goes to 0. This file
// supplies only the Windows platform binding; it is kept identical in shape
// to ../../gcthreadsdarwin and ../../gcthreadslinux so a future divergence
// between the three means a real platform difference, not a spelling one.
import { gcThreadsRun, gcThreadsTotal } from "../gcprops"
import { threadStart, threadRelease } from "../../../../runtime/thread/windows"
import { parkSleepNs, parkMonoNs } from "../../../../runtime/park/windows"

// `gcThreadsRun` takes `sleepFn`/`monoFn` as the generic `(int) => ()` /
// `() => int` shape shared with the Darwin/Linux twins. Windows' own
// `parkSleepNs`/`parkMonoNs` (`runtime/park/windows/wait.bit`) both take a
// caller-owned `*i64` scratch buffer — `parkMonoNs` unlike Darwin's, which
// takes none, but exactly like Linux's — so both are adapted back to the
// shape `gcThreadsRun` expects using this thread's own scratch, which
// nothing else on this OS thread touches concurrently.
let scratch: [2]i64

fn sleepAdapt(ns: int) {
  parkSleepNs(ns, ptrOf(scratch))
}

fn monoAdapt(): int {
  return parkMonoNs(ptrOf(scratch))
}

fn main() {
  let got = gcThreadsRun(threadStart, threadRelease, sleepAdapt, monoAdapt)
  let total = gcThreadsTotal()
  print("${got == total} ${got}\n")
}
